WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.383
SQL injection can let attackers alter database queries and access, modify, or delete application data.

2
00:00:07.383 --> 00:00:14.597
In multi-tenant SaaS systems, injection can threaten both individual customer data and shared platform data.

3
00:00:14.597 --> 00:00:23.198
Command injection can allow remote command execution through diagnostics, file conversion, import jobs, or administrative utilities.

4
00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:27.399
This can turn a normal application feature into a platform compromise path.

5
00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:35.461
NoSQL and LDAP injection follow the same pattern: attacker-controlled input changes the meaning of a query or filter.

6
00:00:35.461 --> 00:00:41.118
Search, identity, directory, and API features need strong input boundaries.

7
00:00:41.118 --> 00:00:46.007
Parameterized queries, input validation, and output encoding work together.

8
00:00:46.007 --> 00:00:54.588
Parameterization separates data from instructions, validation rejects unexpected input, and encoding protects data when it is displayed.

9
00:00:54.588 --> 00:00:58.110
Use parameterized queries and safe APIs.

10
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.164
Validate input against strict expected formats.

11
00:01:01.164 --> 00:01:05.514
Encode output for the correct context and avoid leaking detailed errors.

12
00:01:05.514 --> 00:01:09.715
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.

13
00:01:09.715 --> 00:01:12.858
1 - Vulnerability management; CC7.

14
00:01:12.858 --> 00:01:14.654
2 - Monitoring for anomalies.
